1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,878
One teaspoon of this star would weigh about two billion tonnes.

2
00:00:03,878 --> 00:00:08,725
That's more than every human being on Earth, all eight billion of us, put together.

3
00:00:08,725 --> 00:00:10,360
And here's the strange part.

4
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:15,207
If you could actually bring that teaspoon home, it wouldn't sit quietly on a scale.

5
00:00:15,207 --> 00:00:16,200
It would explode.

6
00:00:16,899 --> 00:00:19,285
So today we're flying to a neutron star.

7
00:00:19,285 --> 00:00:22,923
First, the signal that made astronomers whisper about aliens.

8
00:00:22,923 --> 00:00:26,502
Then how a star gets crushed into a ball the size of a city.

9
00:00:26,502 --> 00:00:27,754
Then we'll get close.

10
00:00:27,754 --> 00:00:39,086
Close enough to see why that teaspoon explodes, why mountains there are smaller than a grain of sand, and why some of the gold on your finger may have come from two of these stars colliding.

11
00:00:39,786 --> 00:00:41,600
A Teaspoon of a Neutron Star.

12
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:44,895
Cambridge, England, nineteen sixty seven.

13
00:00:44,895 --> 00:00:54,518
A graduate student named Jocelyn Bell is checking the output of a new radio telescope, a field of wires and poles the size of fifty seven tennis courts.

14
00:00:54,518 --> 00:00:59,203
It prints its data on paper, about two hundred meters of chart every week.

15
00:00:59,203 --> 00:01:03,825
And in one corner of the sky, she notices a bit of what she calls scruff.

16
00:01:04,525 --> 00:01:06,767
The scruff turns out to be pulses.

17
00:01:06,767 --> 00:01:07,954
Perfectly regular.

18
00:01:07,954 --> 00:01:12,240
One every one point three three seconds, like a cosmic metronome.

19
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:18,637
Nothing natural was known to tick that precisely, so the team half jokingly labeled it L G M one.

20
00:01:18,637 --> 00:01:19,758
Little green men.

21
00:01:19,758 --> 00:01:24,703
But then she found a second one, in a completely different part of the sky.

22
00:01:24,703 --> 00:01:28,924
Two alien civilizations, signaling on the same kind of schedule?

23
00:01:28,924 --> 00:01:29,517
Unlikely.

24
00:01:29,517 --> 00:01:31,429
The real answer was stranger.

25
00:01:31,429 --> 00:01:33,540
These were spinning, dead stars.

26
00:01:33,540 --> 00:01:34,067
Pulsars.

27
00:01:34,067 --> 00:01:41,980
Today we know a few thousand of them, and NASA estimates our galaxy hides tens to hundreds of millions of neutron stars.

28
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:44,021
So how do you make one?

29
00:01:44,021 --> 00:01:47,520
Start with a star at least eight times heavier than the Sun.

30
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:55,274
At the end of its life, its core is a ball of iron about the size of Earth, holding roughly one and a half times the mass of the Sun.

31
00:01:55,274 --> 00:01:57,140
Iron can't be burned for energy.

32
00:01:57,140 --> 00:02:00,813
So when the fuel around it runs out, nothing holds the core up.

33
00:02:01,513 --> 00:02:02,327
It collapses.

34
00:02:02,327 --> 00:02:06,267
The core falls inward at about a quarter of the speed of light.

35
00:02:06,267 --> 00:02:12,521
In half a second or less, a ball the size of a planet becomes a ball about thirty kilometers across.

36
00:02:12,521 --> 00:02:16,399
Electrons are forced into protons, turning them into neutrons.

37
00:02:16,399 --> 00:02:23,029
The outer layers of the star bounce off this new, ultra hard core and blast out into space as a supernova.

38
00:02:23,029 --> 00:02:25,343
What's left behind is a neutron star.

39
00:02:26,043 --> 00:02:27,574
Here's how small that is.

40
00:02:27,574 --> 00:02:33,882
A typical neutron star holds more mass than the Sun, packed into a sphere about twenty kilometers wide.

41
00:02:33,882 --> 00:02:35,046
The size of a city.

42
00:02:35,046 --> 00:02:38,844
Our own Sun is about one point four million kilometers across.

43
00:02:38,844 --> 00:02:46,989
And if you squeezed the whole Earth to the same density, by our calculation it would become a ball about three hundred meters across.

44
00:02:46,989 --> 00:02:48,827
Shorter than the Eiffel Tower.

45
00:02:49,527 --> 00:02:51,213
Now, back to that teaspoon.

46
00:02:51,213 --> 00:02:57,581
By our calculation, an average neutron star is about four hundred million tonnes per cubic centimeter.

47
00:02:57,581 --> 00:03:01,640
A sugar cube of it, NASA says, would weigh around a billion tons.

48
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:03,700
So why would it explode on Earth?

49
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:06,572
Because that density isn't natural for matter.

50
00:03:06,572 --> 00:03:07,322
It's forced.

51
00:03:07,322 --> 00:03:11,880
The star's own gravity is the only thing holding those neutrons together.

52
00:03:11,880 --> 00:03:14,564
Scoop out a teaspoon and that grip is gone.

53
00:03:14,564 --> 00:03:19,372
The pressure inside takes over all at once, and the matter expands violently.

54
00:03:19,372 --> 00:03:28,863
As one astronomer put it, a spoonful of neutron star appearing on Earth would cause a giant explosion, and probably vaporize a good chunk of the planet.

55
00:03:28,863 --> 00:03:31,548
The teaspoon is only heavy while it's home.

56
00:03:32,247 --> 00:03:35,218
So let's leave the teaspoon and go there instead.

57
00:03:35,218 --> 00:03:37,522
Long before you arrive, you'd feel it.

58
00:03:37,522 --> 00:03:39,402
Not the gravity, the magnetism.

59
00:03:39,402 --> 00:03:43,706
When the star collapsed, its magnetic field was squeezed along with it.

60
00:03:43,706 --> 00:03:49,648
NASA says neutron stars have magnetic fields billions to trillions of times stronger than Earth's.

61
00:03:49,648 --> 00:03:54,498
And a special kind, called a magnetar, is about a thousand times stronger still.

62
00:03:55,198 --> 00:03:56,363
How strong is that?

63
00:03:56,363 --> 00:04:03,473
On December twenty seventh, two thousand four, a magnetar called S G R eighteen oh six minus twenty had a starquake.

64
00:04:03,473 --> 00:04:10,645
In about a fifth of a second, it released as much energy as our Sun puts out in two hundred and fifty thousand years.

65
00:04:10,645 --> 00:04:15,427
It was roughly fifty thousand light years away, on the far side of the galaxy.

66
00:04:15,427 --> 00:04:18,430
And it still squeezed Earth's own magnetic field.

67
00:04:19,130 --> 00:04:20,767
Now get close to the surface.

68
00:04:20,767 --> 00:04:26,071
The gravity here is, by our calculation, about a hundred billion times what you feel on Earth.

69
00:04:26,071 --> 00:04:32,053
Drop a pen from one meter up, and it would hit the ground at around sixteen hundred kilometers per second.

70
00:04:32,053 --> 00:04:35,778
To escape, you'd need to fly at more than half the speed of light.

71
00:04:35,778 --> 00:04:38,035
Gravity this strong flattens everything.

72
00:04:38,035 --> 00:04:45,541
Researchers at the University of Southampton calculated that the tallest mountains on a neutron star are less than a millimeter high.

73
00:04:46,241 --> 00:04:48,460
And the view would break your brain.

74
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:50,432
Gravity this strong bends light.

75
00:04:50,432 --> 00:04:55,240
Light leaving the far side of the star curves around it and reaches your eyes.

76
00:04:55,240 --> 00:05:01,280
So from one spot, you would see more than half of the surface at once, including part of the back.

77
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:06,519
NASA's NICER telescope, on the International Space Station, uses exactly this effect.

78
00:05:06,519 --> 00:05:15,025
In twenty nineteen, it mapped hot spots on a pulsar called J zero zero three zero, eleven hundred light years away, and measured its size.

79
00:05:15,025 --> 00:05:20,325
About twenty six kilometers wide, with about one point four times the mass of the Sun.

80
00:05:21,026 --> 00:05:22,807
And underneath that surface?

81
00:05:22,807 --> 00:05:26,305
Nobody has ever seen it, but physicists have a picture.

82
00:05:26,305 --> 00:05:30,122
On top, a thin crust of atomic nuclei locked into a crystal.

83
00:05:30,122 --> 00:05:36,356
Deeper down, the nuclei are squeezed so tightly that they merge into long strands and flat sheets.

84
00:05:36,356 --> 00:05:41,890
Physicists actually call it nuclear pasta, with shapes nicknamed spaghetti and lasagna.

85
00:05:41,890 --> 00:05:51,749
Computer simulations published in twenty eighteen suggest it could be the strongest material in the universe, around ten billion times stronger than steel.

86
00:05:51,749 --> 00:05:53,658
Below that, a sea of neutrons.

87
00:05:53,658 --> 00:05:55,121
And at the very center?

88
00:05:55,121 --> 00:05:56,202
Nobody knows yet.

89
00:05:56,202 --> 00:06:00,337
The matter there is crushed beyond anything we can make in a lab.

90
00:06:01,037 --> 00:06:02,164
And it's spinning.

91
00:06:02,164 --> 00:06:07,614
A collapsing star spins faster as it shrinks, like a figure skater pulling in her arms.

92
00:06:07,614 --> 00:06:11,685
The first pulsar turned once every one point three three seconds.

93
00:06:11,685 --> 00:06:20,392
The fastest one known, discovered in two thousand five in the star cluster Terzan five, turns seven hundred and sixteen times every second.

94
00:06:20,392 --> 00:06:23,586
Its beams sweep past us like a lighthouse gone mad.

95
00:06:24,286 --> 00:06:30,044
NASA compares it to forty three thousand turns per minute, twice as fast as a kitchen blender.

96
00:06:30,044 --> 00:06:32,922
Except this blender has more mass than the Sun.

97
00:06:32,922 --> 00:06:38,802
By our calculation, a point on its equator moves at around fifty thousand kilometers per second.

98
00:06:38,802 --> 00:06:41,558
That's roughly a fifth of the speed of light.

99
00:06:41,558 --> 00:06:45,662
Spin it much faster, and the star would start to tear itself apart.

100
00:06:46,362 --> 00:06:48,061
Now the biggest scale of all.

101
00:06:48,061 --> 00:06:50,697
Sometimes two neutron stars orbit each other.

102
00:06:50,697 --> 00:06:55,792
They slowly spiral together, and when they finally hit, the universe rings like a bell.

103
00:06:55,792 --> 00:07:01,474
On August seventeenth, twenty seventeen, gravitational wave detectors on Earth felt that ringing.

104
00:07:01,474 --> 00:07:09,264
Two neutron stars had collided in a galaxy called N G C forty nine ninety three, about a hundred and thirty million light years away.

105
00:07:09,264 --> 00:07:12,837
The ripple had been traveling since the age of the dinosaurs.

106
00:07:13,537 --> 00:07:18,664
Within hours, telescopes around the world turned to look, and they saw the fireball.

107
00:07:18,664 --> 00:07:21,289
It was full of freshly made heavy elements.

108
00:07:21,289 --> 00:07:29,468
Harvard astronomer Edo Berger estimated that this one collision produced about ten times the mass of Earth in gold and platinum alone.

109
00:07:29,468 --> 00:07:33,985
Collisions like this are now thought to forge much of the universe's gold.

110
00:07:33,985 --> 00:07:40,089
Billions of years ago, some of that gold drifted into the cloud of gas that became our solar system.

111
00:07:40,089 --> 00:07:45,765
So if you're wearing gold, some of it may have been made by two dead stars smashing together.

112
00:07:46,466 --> 00:07:47,887
So that's a neutron star.

113
00:07:47,887 --> 00:07:57,840
A city sized ball, heavier than the Sun, spinning hundreds of times a second, where a teaspoon outweighs all of humanity, and only stays that way because it never leaves home.

114
00:07:57,840 --> 00:08:01,821
Now picture something much closer, and much heavier than any teaspoon.

115
00:08:01,821 --> 00:08:05,177
Our Moon weighs about seventy three billion billion tonnes.

116
00:08:05,177 --> 00:08:07,679
In the last episode we saw it drifting away.

117
00:08:07,679 --> 00:08:10,466
But what if it came back, and crashed into Earth?

118
00:08:10,466 --> 00:08:11,148
That's next.
